15. Full Route Cache is still disabled
Later optimization
The root layout and public page routes still use:
export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic';


This does not mean your pages are client-only. SSR is still working.

It means Next.js does not reuse a cached, fully rendered route response in the normal Full Route Cache.
Explain it simply
Your kitchen remembers some ingredients, but it still prepares the whole meal again for every customer.

That is what happens here:
- Categories can come from Data Cache.
- Locations can come from Data Cache.
- Settings can come from Data Cache.
- Listing results are fetched from PostgreSQL.
- The page is rendered again for each request.
What needs to be done
After listing-result caching and invalidation are reliable, assess which public pages can safely use route-level caching or ISR.

Do not blindly remove force-dynamic from everything. Search pages and filtered pages depend on request-specific query parameters.

The homepage and suitable public routes can be evaluated separately.

Also, invalidateLiveListingCountsCache() currently calls revalidatePath('/', 'layout'). That is a broad route invalidation. Once route caching is introduced, make sure listing-count changes do not unnecessarily invalidate every route when a more precise cache-tag invalidation will do.

Source: app/layout.tsx, reference-data.cache.ts.

16. PostgreSQL pool settings have not changed
Measure first
The current pool still has:
max: 10,
idleTimeoutMillis: 2000,
connectionTimeoutMillis: 10000,


The earlier suggestion to increase idle timeout to 30 seconds has not been implemented.

A two-second idle timeout can cause more connection turnover. But a longer timeout also keeps more database connections open.

I would not change this blindly.

First measure:
- How many database connections are open.
- How often connections are created and closed.
- Whether requests wait for a connection.
- How many application instances are running.
- PostgreSQL's maximum connection allowance.
Source: src/db/index.ts.

